Phase 4 checkpoint: Induced Failure in inventory-service

Automated tests: sbt inventoryService/test (9/9), orderService/test (21/21),
purerest/test (23/23).

Manual verification (prompting off): scripts/verify-resilience-end-to-end.sh runs the
real stack against a genuinely flaky inventory-service (not just stub Client[F]s).

Real finding: the first version, using a 60% induced failure rate for the
"retry masks transient failures" demo, accidentally tripped the shared circuit
breaker partway through its own 10-order loop (order-service's hardcoded
CircuitBreakerConfig(failureThreshold=5, resetTimeout=30s) — enough attempts across
10 orders' worth of retries produced 5 consecutive failures). Once tripped, the
breaker rejected almost everything for the rest of the 30s reset window, which has
nothing to do with retry's own effectiveness and swamped the measurement (2/10
succeeded instead of the expected ~9-10/10). Not a code bug — a genuine
resilience-tuning interaction between the two mechanisms' configured thresholds.
Fixed by lowering the demo's failure rate to 30% (P(one order exhausts all 4
attempts) ≈0.8% at 30% vs ≈13% at 60%, keeping the breaker safely closed during this
part of the demo).

Final results: at 30% induced failure, 10/10 orders succeeded via retry. At 100%
induced failure, request latency dropped from ~1065ms (full retry+backoff exhausted)
to ~22ms (circuit breaker open, fails fast) by the 3rd request — a clean, convincing
demonstration of both mechanisms working as designed. All checks passed.
